The wheel handler can interrupt vertical-first scrolling, and the new tests depend on uncontrolled timing. Typecheck and focused lint passed; browser execution was blocked by sandbox launch restrictions.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Latch vertical wheel gestures until the quiet interval — /Users/asmirnou/Projects/g/ramsider/components/TechnologyViewer.tsx:171-173
  When a trackpad gesture starts vertically and then bends horizontally, this branch clears the distance but leaves navigation enabled. For example, `(deltaX: 0, deltaY: 120)` followed 30 ms later by `(-60, 0)` switches to CyberMind and resets the ribbon position, interrupting the ongoing vertical scroll. Unlike `onTouchMove`, the wheel handler does not reject vertical-first trajectories. Track the gesture's axis and suppress navigation until the quiet interval ends.

- [P2] Control elapsed time in wheel-burst tests — /Users/asmirnou/Projects/g/ramsider/tests/e2e/technology-trackpad.spec.ts:26-27
  On a slow browser or busy runner, the assertion between these wheel events can take longer than the handler's 180 ms quiet interval, so the second event starts a new gesture and never reaches the accumulated threshold. The reversal test has the same problem because it waits for image readiness before sending its supposedly same-burst reversal. These tests can therefore fail with correct behavior; send burst events under controlled timing and assert afterward.
